"# How the pieces fit\n\nThe mental model: daemon = hardware, stack = memory, workspace = external memory, definition = source code, kernel = gatekeeper.\n\nThink of a small computer:\n\n| aos word | like | where |\n|---|---|---|\n| daemon | the hardware; it runs procs and keeps the table of who is running | `$AOS_DAEMON` (the daemon's own stack folder) |\n| stack | RAM of one running proc: its state, history, mailbox | `$AOS_STACK` |\n| workspace (ws) | the disk you work on; shared with other agents when they are given the same ws | `$AOS_WS` |\n| definition | the program's source code; running it makes an instance (a stack under a daemon); its files: `aos/definition.json` | the folder an agent was started from |\n| kernel | the gatekeeper: decides if a proc may run; the daemon is the simplest one (it says yes to everything) | `$AOS_KERNEL` when a judge is in front of the daemon |\n| llm kernel | a separate daemon that answers brain requests sent as letters | `$AOS_LLM_KERNEL` (its stack; letters go to `$AOS_LLM_KERNEL/requests/`) |\n| knowledge base | a folder tree of facts. You read the stock one with `knowledge_read` (you get its root at the start). To write, you need one of your own: `knowledge_write` with `root`, or `$AOS_KNOWLEDGE` set up for you; see `aos/knowledge.json` | `$AOS_KNOWLEDGE` (only when set up for you) |\n\nFacts that follow from the model:\n\n- One definition can run as many instances. Each instance has its own stack. Changing a stack never changes the definition.\n- Do not edit a definition by hand. To make a new one, use `agent_def` (it copies one first). Per-instance changes (your tools, your brain settings) live in your stack as an \"overlay\": only the differences are stored.\n- A proc is always under exactly one daemon. \"My daemon\" is the one in `$AOS_DAEMON`. Subagents you start go on the same daemon.\n- Everything persists as files, so a daemon can sleep, be copied elsewhere with its stacks and workspace, and wake up there.\n- Environment variables set for you: `AOS_STACK` (your stack), `AOS_DAEMON` (your daemon), `AOS_WS` (your workspace, when you have one), `AOS_HOME` (where proto8 is; definitions find tools through it). When set up for you: `AOS_LLM_KERNEL`, `AOS_KERNEL`, `AOS_KNOWLEDGE`, `AOS_CLOCK`, `AOS_CLOCK_INTERVAL`, `AOS_DO_TASK_FROM` (default definition for `do_task`), `AOS_DO_TASK_DEPTH`. A tool also sees `AOS_CD` (its own folder). Do not invent others.\n\n## Time\n\n- The tree has a clock like a board's crystal: when `AOS_CLOCK` is set, it names a file holding one integer, the current cycle. The root daemon advances it with an ordinary `_clock` row in its `procs`; sub-daemons normally have no clock row and follow the root's clock.\n- Between procs, write waits in cycles: `do_task` and `subagent` ask take `timeout_cycles` instead of `timeout_s` (give one, not both). With a clock, a wait ends after that many cycles; if the clock is paused or the tree sleeps, time does not pass and the wait does not expire.\n- No clock (`AOS_CLOCK` not set): cycles fall back to wall time, 1 cycle = `AOS_CLOCK_INTERVAL` ms (default 1000).\n- Waits for people, for outside services and for Linux processes stay in seconds or milliseconds.\n- Timers: clock letters (tool `clock_register`) arrive in your queue as messages; a `on_clock` hook only runs a program and adds nothing to your history (agent templates come with a `timer` hook every 5 minutes that does nothing). See aos/clock.json.\n- Cycle numbers only compare inside one tree; in letters to another tree, use milliseconds.\n"